     "answer": "Majoritarianism in Sri Lanka refers to the dominance of the Sinhalese community in politics and policy-making. After independence, the Sinhalese leaders adopted a series of measures to establish Sinhala supremacy. In 1956, an Act was passed to recognize Sinhala as the only official language, disregarding Tamil. The government also favored Sinhalese applicants for university positions and government jobs. These measures alienated the Tamil community, leading to the formation of militant groups demanding a separate state. The civil war that followed lasted for nearly three decades, causing immense loss of life and property. The consequences of majoritarianism in Sri Lanka include ethnic conflict, civil war, displacement of people, and economic instability."
