     "answer": "Coming-together federations are formed when independent states come together on their own to form a bigger unit. In such federations, the central government tends to have more power and the constituent states have less autonomy. The states have the right to secede from the federation. An example is the United States of America where 13 independent states came together to form a federal government. Holding-together federations are formed when a large country decides to divide its power between the central government and state governments. In such federations, the central government is usually more powerful and the states have limited autonomy. The states do not have the right to secede. An example is India where the central government holds more power and the states are subordinate to it. Most federations in the world are holding-together federations."
