     "answer": "The Union List contains subjects on which only the central government can make laws. These subjects are of national importance. Examples include defence, foreign affairs, banking, currency, and communications. The State List contains subjects on which only state governments can make laws. These subjects are of local importance. Examples include police, public health, agriculture, and irrigation. The Concurrent List contains subjects on which both central and state governments can make laws. Examples include education, marriage, divorce, bankruptcy, and forests. If there is a conflict between central and state laws on a concurrent subject, the central law prevails. This division of powers ensures that both levels of government can function effectively within their respective jurisdictions."
